{% set local_problem %}
<p>Downspouts are often overlooked, but on Arab's hilly terrain, they're critical to protecting your home. When your downspout deposits water, it needs to flow away from your foundation — not toward it. On a slope, water that starts at your foundation gains velocity as it flows downhill, concentrating erosion and increasing hydrostatic pressure.</p>

<p>We see Arab homes all the time with downspouts that were clearly installed without considering the terrain. Water dumps at the foundation's uphill side, flows down around the house, and collects at the low point — often right where a basement or crawl space entrance is located.</p>

<p>Other common problems: undersized 2x3 downspouts that overflow during heavy rain, missing or damaged extensions that let water pool at the foundation, downspouts that have separated from gutters due to age or storm damage, and downspouts spaced 50-60+ feet apart when they should be every 35-40 feet.</p>

<p><strong>Before spending $2,000+ on new gutters:</strong> Check if adding downspouts would solve your overflow problems. Sometimes a home has decent gutters but not enough downspouts — the water has nowhere to go, so it overflows. Adding 1-2 downspouts at $75-150 each might fix the problem for $300 instead of thousands.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Proper downspout installation on hillside properties requires planning that goes beyond just connecting a pipe to your gutter. We consider:</p>

<p><strong>Terrain Analysis:</strong> We assess your lot's slope and identify the natural direction water wants to flow. Then we position downspouts to work with gravity, not against it.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity Matching:</strong> Downspouts must be sized to handle your gutter's water volume. An undersized 2x3 downspout on a 6-inch gutter creates a bottleneck that causes gutters to overflow — even when gutters are clean. We use 3x4 downspouts that handle twice the flow rate.</p>

<p><strong>Proper Spacing:</strong> One downspout every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run. That's more frequent than the 50-60+ feet we often see on older homes and builder-grade installations. More downspouts means each one handles less volume, reducing backup risk.</p>

<p><strong>Extension Strategy:</strong> On sloped lots, downspouts often need to deposit water 10+ feet from the foundation — sometimes more. We configure extensions or underground connections to achieve safe discharge distances.</p>

<p><strong>Aesthetic Integration:</strong> Downspouts should protect your home without being eyesores. We match colors, position strategically, and minimize visual impact while maximizing function.</p>
{% endset %}

